747	<body>
748	
749	<header>
750	    <h1>Global Airbnb Market Comparison</h1>
751	    <p>Pricing, Supply &amp; Guest Demand Across 10 Major Cities</p>
752	    <p style="margin-top:14px; max-width:700px; font-size:0.95em;">A comprehensive cross-city analysis of Airbnb listings across New York, London, Paris, Tokyo, Barcelona, Los Angeles, Amsterdam, Bangkok, Rome, and Sydney — examining pricing dynamics, room-type distribution, host concentration, and guest engagement patterns.</p>
753	    <div class="meta">Data source: Inside Airbnb &bull; Scrape date: March 29, 2026 &bull; {sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()):,} listings analyzed</div>
754	</header>
755	
756	<div class="container">
757	
758	<!-- ── SECTION 1: Market Overview ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
759	<div class="section" id="market-overview">
760	    <h2>1. Global Market Overview</h2>
761	    <p class="objective">Summarize the total number of listings per city, overall price distribution, and room-type breakdown to establish the scale and composition of each market.</p>
762	
763	    <p>The dataset encompasses <strong>{sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()):,} Airbnb listings</strong> across 10 major global cities. Listing counts range widely — from <strong>Bangkok</strong> at {stats['Bangkok']['total_listings']:,} to <strong>{max_listings_city}</strong> at {stats[max_listings_city]['total_listings']:,} — reflecting differences in market maturity, regulatory environments, and city size.</p>
764	
765	    <div class="chart-container">
766	        {charts['listing_counts']}
767	    </div>
768	
769	    <p>The room-type mix reveals clear levels of market commercialization. <strong>{max_entire_city}</strong> leads with entire-home rentals at <strong>{stats[max_entire_city]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[max_entire_city]['total_listings'] * 100:.1f}%</strong> of its supply, while cities with lower entire-home shares reflect more traditional homeshare dynamics. Shared rooms remain a negligible share across all markets (typically &lt;3%).</p>
770	
771	    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Room Type Breakdown by City</h3>
772	    <table>
773	        <thead><tr>
774	            <th>City</th><th>Total Listings</th>
775	            <th>Entire Home/Apt</th><th>Private Room</th><th>Shared Room</th><th>Hotel Room</th>
776	        </tr></thead>
777	        <tbody>{room_overview_rows}</tbody>
778	    </table>
779	
780	    <div class="chart-container">
781	        {charts['room_type_dist']}
782	    </div>
783	
784	    <div class="fact-box">
785	        <strong>Key observation:</strong> The two largest markets by volume — <strong>London</strong> ({stats['London']['total_listings']:,}) and <strong>Paris</strong> ({stats['Paris']['total_listings']:,}) — together account for <strong>{ (stats['London']['total_listings'] + stats['Paris']['total_listings']) / sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()) * 100:.1f}%</strong> of all listings in the dataset. However, their pricing profiles diverge significantly, as detailed in the next section.
786	    </div>
787	</div>
788	
789	<!-- ── SECTION 2: Pricing Analysis ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
790	<div class="section" id="pricing-analysis">
791	    <h2>2. Pricing Dynamics Across Cities</h2>
792	    <p class="objective">Compare median and average nightly prices by city and room type, identify the most and least expensive markets, and highlight price dispersion within each city.</p>
793	
794	    <h3>Price Rankings (Median Nightly Rate, USD)</h3>
795	    <p style="margin:10px 0;">
796	        {" &gt; ".join(f'<span class="rank-badge rank-{i+1}">#{i+1}</span> {c} (${stats[c]["price_median"]:.0f})' for i, c in enumerate(price_rank))}
797	    </p>
798	
799	    <div class="chart-container">
800	        {charts['price_median']}
801	    </div>
802	
803	    <p><strong>{price_rank[0]}</strong> commands the highest median price at <strong>${stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median']:.0f}/night</strong>, positioning it as the premium market. At the opposite end, <strong>{price_rank[-1]}</strong> is the most affordable at <strong>${stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median']:.0f}/night</strong> — a {stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median'] / stats[price_rank[-1]]['price_median']:.1f}x difference between the top and bottom markets. Mean prices are consistently higher than medians across all cities (indicating right-skewed distributions with luxury-tier outliers), with the gap most pronounced in <strong>Bangkok</strong> and <strong>Los Angeles</strong>.</p>
804	
805	    <div class="chart-container">
806	        {charts['price_boxplot']}
807	    </div>
808	
809	    <p>The boxplot above (outliers above $1,000 excluded for readability) reveals significant price dispersion within each market. <strong>{max(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: (stats[c]['price_p75'] or 0) - (stats[c]['price_p25'] or 0))}</strong> shows the widest interquartile range, while <strong>{min(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: (stats[c]['price_p75'] or 0) - (stats[c]['price_p25'] or 0))}</strong> has the most compressed pricing.</p>
810	
811	    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Median Price by Room Type Across Cities</h3>
812	    <div class="chart-container">
813	        {charts['price_by_room_type']}
814	    </div>
815	
816	    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Room-Type Price Matrix (USD)</h3>
817	    <table>
818	        <thead><tr>
819	            <th>City</th><th>Room Type</th><th>Count</th><th>Median Price</th><th>Mean Price</th>
820	        </tr></thead>
821	        <tbody>{room_price_details}</tbody>
822	    </table>
823	
824	    <div class="fact-box">
825	        <strong>Investor insight:</strong> The entire-home premium — the gap between entire-home and private-room median prices — varies dramatically by city. This gap signals the marginal return on offering a full unit versus a shared space, and is widest in cities with strong tourist demand and constrained hotel supply.
826	    </div>
827	
828	    <p class="note">Note: {sum(s['price_missing'] for s in stats.values()):,} listings ({sum(s['price_missing'] for s in stats.values()) / sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()) * 100:.1f}%) had missing price values and were excluded from price calculations.</p>
829	</div>
