}}
.note {{ font-size: 0.85em; color: #888; margin-top: 8px; }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
    <h1>Global Airbnb Market Comparison</h1>
    <p>Pricing, Supply &amp; Guest Demand Across 10 Major Cities</p>
    <p style="margin-top:14px; max-width:700px; font-size:0.95em;">A comprehensive cross-city analysis of Airbnb listings across New York, London, Paris, Tokyo, Barcelona, Los Angeles, Amsterdam, Bangkok, Rome, and Sydney — examining pricing dynamics, room-type distribution, host concentration, and guest engagement patterns.</p>
    <div class="meta">Data source: Inside Airbnb &bull; Scrape date: March 29, 2026 &bull; {sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()):,} listings analyzed</div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- ── SECTION 1: Market Overview ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="section" id="market-overview">
    <h2>1. Global Market Overview</h2>
    <p class="objective">Summarize the total number of listings per city, overall price distribution, and room-type breakdown to establish the scale and composition of each market.</p>

    <p>The dataset encompasses <strong>{sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()):,} Airbnb listings</strong> across 10 major global cities. Listing counts range widely — from <strong>Bangkok</strong> at {stats['Bangkok']['total_listings']:,} to <strong>{max_listings_city}</strong> at {stats[max_listings_city]['total_listings']:,} — reflecting differences in market maturity, regulatory environments, and city size.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['listing_counts']}
    </div>

    <p>The room-type mix reveals clear levels of market commercialization. <strong>{max_entire_city}</strong> leads with entire-home rentals at <strong>{stats[max_entire_city]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[max_entire_city]['total_listings'] * 100:.1f}%</strong> of its supply, while cities with lower entire-home shares reflect more traditional homeshare dynamics. Shared rooms remain a negligible share across all markets (typically &lt;3%).</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Room Type Breakdown by City</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr>
            <th>City</th><th>Total Listings</th>
            <th>Entire Home/Apt</th><th>Private Room</th><th>Shared Room</th><th>Hotel Room</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>{room_overview_rows}</tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['room_type_dist']}
    </div>

    <div class="fact-box">
        <strong>Key observation:</strong> The two largest markets by volume — <strong>London</strong> ({stats['London']['total_listings']:,}) and <strong>Paris</strong> ({stats['Paris']['total_listings']:,}) — together account for <strong>{ (stats['London']['total_listings'] + stats['Paris']['total_listings']) / sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()) * 100:.1f}%</strong> of all listings in the dataset. However, their pricing profiles diverge significantly, as detailed in the next section.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ── SECTION 2: Pricing Analysis ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="section" id="pricing-analysis">
    <h2>2. Pricing Dynamics Across Cities</h2>
    <p class="objective">Compare median and average nightly prices by city and room type, identify the most and least expensive markets, and highlight price dispersion within each city.</p>

    <h3>Price Rankings (Median Nightly Rate, USD)</h3>
    <p style="margin:10px 0;">
        {" &gt; ".join(f'<span class="rank-badge rank-{i+1}">#{i+1}</span> {c} (${stats[c]["price_median"]:.0f})' for i, c in enumerate(price_rank))}
    </p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['price_median']}
    </div>

    <p><strong>{price_rank[0]}</strong> commands the highest median price at <strong>${stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median']:.0f}/night</strong>, positioning it as the premium market. At the opposite end, <strong>{price_rank[-1]}</strong> is the most affordable at <strong>${stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median']:.0f}/night</strong> — a {stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median'] / stats[price_rank[-1]]['price_median']:.1f}x difference between the top and bottom markets. Mean prices are consistently higher than medians across all cities (indicating right-skewed distributions with luxury-tier outliers), with the gap most pronounced in <strong>Bangkok</strong> and <strong>Los Angeles</strong>.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['price_boxplot']}
    </div>

    <p>The boxplot above (outliers above $1,000 excluded for readability) reveals significant price dispersion within each market. <strong>{max(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: (stats[c]['price_p75'] or 0) - (stats[c]['price_p25'] or 0))}</strong> shows the widest interquartile range, while <strong>{min(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: (stats[c]['price_p75'] or 0) - (stats[c]['price_p25'] or 0))}</strong> has the most compressed pricing.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Median Price by Room Type Across Cities</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['price_by_room_type']}
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Room-Type Price Matrix (USD)</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr>
            <th>City</th><th>Room Type</th><th>Count</th><th>Median Price</th><th>Mean Price</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>{room_price_details}</tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="fact-box">
        <strong>Investor insight:</strong> The entire-home premium — the gap between entire-home and private-room median prices — varies dramatically by city. This gap signals the marginal return on offering a full unit versus a shared space, and is widest in cities with strong tourist demand and constrained hotel supply.
    </div>

    <p class="note">Note: {sum(s['price_missing'] for s in stats.values()):,} listings ({sum(s['price_missing'] for s in stats.values()) / sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()) * 100:.1f}%) had missing price values and were excluded from price calculations.</p>
</div>

<!-- ── SECTION 3: Supply Structure ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="section" id="supply-structure">
    <h2>3. Supply Structure &amp; Host Concentration</h2>
    <p class="objective">Analyze the distribution of room types per city, examine host listing counts to assess professional vs. individual host prevalence, and identify cities with the highest host concentration.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['pro_hosts']}
    </div>

    <p>Professional hosts — defined as hosts with <strong>5 or more listings</strong> — represent a strategically critical dimension of market structure. High professional-host concentration indicates an investable, operator-driven market; low concentration signals a more fragmented, individual-host landscape with different competitive dynamics and regulatory risk profiles.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Professional Host Concentration by City</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr>
            <th>City</th><th>Total Listings</th><th>Pro Host Listings (5+)</th><th>Pro Host Share</th><th>Avg Listings/Host</th>
            <th>Entire Home Share</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            {"".join(f"<tr><td style='font-weight:600'>{c}</td><td>{stats[c]['total_listings']:,}</td><td>{stats[c]['pro_host_count']:,}</td><td>{stats[c]['pro_host_share']:.1f}%</td><td>{stats[c]['host_listings_mean']:.1f}</td><td>{stats[c]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[c]['total_listings'] * 100:.1f}%</td></tr>" for c in CITIES_ORDER)}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <ul style="margin:20px 0;">
        {"".join(pro_share_strs)}
    </ul>

    <p><strong>{max_pro_city}</strong> has the highest professional-host concentration at <strong>{stats[max_pro_city]['pro_host_share']:.1f}%</strong>, nearly <strong>{stats[max_pro_city]['pro_host_share'] / max(0.1, stats[min_pro_city]['pro_host_share']):.1f}x</strong> the level of <strong>{min_pro_city}</strong> ({stats[min_pro_city]['pro_host_share']:.1f}%). Markets with high professionalization tend to be more scalable from an operator perspective but also face greater regulatory scrutiny — an important consideration for market-entry strategies.</p>

    <div class="fact-box">
        <strong>Regulatory watch:</strong> Cities with high entire-home shares coupled with high professional-host concentration (like <strong>{max_pro_city}</strong>) are most exposed to short-term rental regulations targeting commercial operators. Investors should monitor local policy environments closely in these markets.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ── SECTION 4: Guest Demand ───────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="section" id="guest-demand">
    <h2>4. Guest Demand &amp; Review Activity</h2>
    <p class="objective">Use review metrics as demand proxies to compare guest engagement across cities and room types, and identify recency patterns.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['reviews_per_month']}
    </div>

    <p>Reviews per month serves as the strongest available proxy for booking frequency. <strong>{max_reviews_city}</strong> leads with a mean of <strong>{stats[max_reviews_city]['reviews_pm_mean']:.2f} reviews/month</strong>, suggesting strong and consistent demand. The gap between mean and median review rates across all cities indicates a subset of high-performing listings drive outsized review volumes — likely professionally managed units with optimized pricing and availability.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['review_recency']}
    </div>

    <p>Review recency patterns confirm that the majority of listings with review activity have been reviewed within the past 12 months. <strong>{max(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['review_recent_3m_pct'])}</strong> shows the highest share of recently-reviewed listings ({stats[max(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['review_recent_3m_pct'])]['review_recent_3m_pct']:.1f}% within 3 months), indicating an active and current booking base.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:25px;">Demand Indicators Summary</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr>
            <th>City</th><th>Avg Reviews/Mo</th><th>Median Reviews/Mo</th>
            <th>Avg Total Reviews</th><th>Avg Reviews (LTM)</th>
            <th>3-Mo Review Recency</th><th>12-Mo Review Recency</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            {"".join(f"<tr><td style='font-weight:600'>{c}</td><td>{stats[c]['reviews_pm_mean']:.2f}</td><td>{stats[c]['reviews_pm_median']:.2f}</td><td>{stats[c]['reviews_total_mean']:.0f}</td><td>{stats[c]['reviews_ltm_mean']:.1f}</td><td>{stats[c]['review_recent_3m_pct']:.1f}%</td><td>{stats[c]['review_recent_12m'] / stats[c]['total_listings'] * 100:.1f}%</td></tr>" for c in CITIES_ORDER)}
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<!-- ── SECTION 5: Availability & Booking Constraints ──────────────────────── -->
<div class="section" id="availability-minimumnights">
    <h2>5. Availability &amp; Booking Constraints</h2>
    <p class="objective">Examine availability and minimum-night distributions across cities to understand listing accessibility and potential regulatory impacts.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['availability']}
    </div>

    <p>Mean availability across cities ranges from <strong>{min(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['avail_mean'] or 999):.0f}</strong> to <strong>{stats[max_avail_city]['avail_mean']:.0f} days/year</strong>. High availability (&gt;250 days) typically signals two possible dynamics: (1) a market with lower occupancy and weaker demand, or (2) a market with a large share of dedicated short-term rental inventory (as opposed to occasionally-rented primary residences). <strong>{max_avail_city}</strong>'s high mean availability ({stats[max_avail_city]['avail_mean']:.0f} days) aligns with its high professional-host share, suggesting the latter.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['minimum_nights']}
    </div>

    <p>Minimum-night requirements are a critical signal of regulatory influence. Cities with high mean minimum nights often reflect local short-term rental caps (e.g., 30-night or 90-night annual limits), which operators comply with by setting long minimum stays. <strong>Bangkok</strong> stands out with a mean minimum of {stats['Bangkok']['min_nights_mean']:.1f} nights — likely driven by a combination of regulatory frameworks and long-stay tourist patterns in Southeast Asia.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        {charts['price_vs_avail']}
    </div>

    <p>The price-availability scatterplot above reveals market archetypes. The upper-right quadrant (high price, high availability) represents commercially-oriented, premium markets. The lower-left (low price, low availability) signals more residential, occasional-hosting markets. The bubble sizes reflect total listings, giving a sense of market scale alongside positioning.</p>
</div>

<!-- ── SECTION 6: Key Takeaways ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<div class="section" id="key-takeaways">
    <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>
    <p class="objective">Synthesize findings into actionable insights for investors, hosts, and policymakers.</p>

    <ol class="takeaway-list">
        <li>
            <strong>Premium pricing power is concentrated in English-speaking and Western European gateway cities.</strong>
            {" " + price_rank[0]} (${stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median']:.0f}), {" " + price_rank[1]} (${stats[price_rank[1]]['price_median']:.0f}), and {" " + price_rank[2]} (${stats[price_rank[2]]['price_median']:.0f}) top the median-price rankings. These markets command a significant premium over Asian destinations — {" " + price_rank[0]} is <strong>{stats[price_rank[0]]['price_median'] / stats[price_rank[-1]]['price_median']:.1f}x</strong> more expensive per night than {" " + price_rank[-1]}. For investors seeking revenue-per-unit maximization, these remain the top-tier markets — but with correspondingly high barriers to entry.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Professional host concentration defines market character and regulatory exposure.</strong>
            {max_pro_city} ({stats[max_pro_city]['pro_host_share']:.1f}%) and {sorted(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['pro_host_share'], reverse=True)[1]} ({stats[sorted(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['pro_host_share'], reverse=True)[1]]['pro_host_share']:.1f}%) have the highest professional-host shares, making them attractive for scaled operators but also the most exposed to anti-commercialization regulations. At the other extreme, {min_pro_city} ({stats[min_pro_city]['pro_host_share']:.1f}%) remains dominated by individual hosts — lower operational complexity but harder to scale.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Entire-home dominance signals commercialization; private-room strength signals authentic homeshare.</strong>
            {max_entire_city} ({stats[max_entire_city]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[max_entire_city]['total_listings'] * 100:.1f}% entire-home) and {sorted(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[c]['total_listings'] * 100, reverse=True)[1]} ({stats[sorted(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[c]['total_listings'] * 100, reverse=True)[1]]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[sorted(CITIES_ORDER, key=lambda c: stats[c]['room_counts'].get('Entire home/apt', 0) / stats[c]['total_listings'] * 100, reverse=True)[1]]['total_listings'] * 100:.1f}%) are the most commercialized markets by room-type mix. Cities with balanced private-room shares (&gt;35%) represent a different value proposition: lower ADR but potentially more regulatory resilience and local community alignment.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Guest demand, proxied by review activity, is strongest in {max_reviews_city}.</strong>
            At {stats[max_reviews_city]['reviews_pm_mean']:.2f} average reviews per month, {max_reviews_city} leads all cities in guest engagement. Review recency data shows {stats[max_reviews_city]['review_recent_3m_pct']:.1f}% of its listings were reviewed within the last 3 months. For investors seeking cash-flow velocity (frequent bookings, consistent turnover), high-review-velocity markets merit priority consideration.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>High availability coupled with high prices signals dedicated STR inventory, not weak demand.</strong>
            {max_avail_city} demonstrates the archetype: {stats[max_avail_city]['avail_mean']:.0f} mean available days/year alongside a median price of ${stats[max_avail_city]['price_median']:.0f}. This combination — high availability AND strong pricing — is the hallmark of a professionalized market with purpose-built short-term rental supply, rather than a market with low occupancy. Investors should read availability in context with price and professional-host share, not in isolation.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Minimum-night requirements are early-warning signals for regulatory constraints.</strong>
            Cities with elevated minimum-night means (notably Bangkok at {stats['Bangkok']['min_nights_mean']:.1f} nights) warrant careful regulatory due diligence. Long minimum stays often reflect local ordinances capping annual rental nights — operators compensate by requiring longer individual stays. Before entering any market, cross-reference minimum-night data with local STR ordinances.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The dataset has data-quality gaps that affect certain analyses.</strong>
            {sum(s['price_missing'] for s in stats.values()):,} listings ({sum(s['price_missing'] for s in stats.values()) / sum(s['total_listings'] for s in stats.values()) * 100:.1f}%) lack pricing data, with missing rates varying by city. Paris and Bangkok have notably higher missing-price rates. Additionally, some cities have significant zero-review listing inventories, which may represent inactive or newly-listed properties. These gaps should be factored into market-sizing and revenue projections.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h3 style="margin-top:30px;">KPI Summary Dashboard</h3>
    <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        {format_kpi_rows(kpi_rows)}
    </div>
</div>

</div>

<footer>
